ble on  the medical marijuana issue on pushing  DEA raids in California in other states  what's going on with that and is he  losing people on the left because of his  medical  marijuana steps yeah he's certainly  losing people on the left because he's  just been flip-flopping all around and  if I had to actually give you my opinion  as to what I think is really going on  it's that you know Obama is the CEO of  what is essentially the largest company  in the country called the federal  government and so it's hard to actually  manage millions of people simultaneously  and so what he's doing because he and  his chief of staff have limited time  they're only focusing on the big issues  but Eric Holder said early on that they  weren't going to be doing this type of  stuff yeah I'm not saying there hasn't  been a flip-flop I'm skeptical that  Obama is sort of scheming actively on  this I think what's happening is that  it's not a high priority issue and it's  not paying attention and so then these  bureaucrats who are running the DEA and  so forth are getting to call their own  shots in 2010 California had a marijuana  legalization ballot initiative which got  a fairly surprising forty seven percent  of the vote was that a good thing for  marijuana legalization and what are the  prospects come 2012 I think it was not a  good thing for anyone who donated a lot  of money to that campaign but for those  of us that donated zero dollars that  campaign it was a good thing because it  did move the ball forward you know  getting forty six and a half percent is  the high water mark for any initiative  in the history of the country to seek  the end of marijuana prohibition what  about in other states like Colorado is  looking to push something like that  possibly Nevada a couple other places do  you think that what are the prospects of  a marijuana legalization ballot  initiative or bill actually becoming law  in 2012 well the Colorado initiative  that you mentioned is actually our  flagship project so I actually think  that it's more likely than not that  we're going to be able to pass that  initiative in Colorado the reason I say  that is that the polling Colorado on  this initiative is currently at about  fifty percent and support is increasing  in Colorado over time combined with that  is a Colorado is actually a swing state  in the presidential election when you  have a huge voter turnout in a swing  state during a presidential election  that means that there's a lot of young  people coming out to vote who otherwise  wouldn't vote and that's actually good  for any marijuana initiative in addition  we're going to have money on our side  and the opposition is probably not going  to have real money on their side which  is the case basically with every  statewide marijuana initiative I guess  the hardest thing in that is going to be  putting up with all the Rocky Mountain  High  drugs well you know I think the people  of Colorado already used to this joke so  we might actually be impervious okay  well that's very good are there other  ballot initiatives or law you know  initiatives that you're looking forward  to as we go into the I watermark of the  presidential election it's highly likely  there's going to be a initiative to end  marijuana prohibition in Washington  State there might be another try in  California but it's unclear if the money  is going to be there and then in terms  of medical marijuana the state that's  most likely to do an initiative is  Arkansas and that's just a question of  whether we could quickly raise a hundred  thousand dollars for that and then Idaho  would be also another medical marijuana  initiative state so we're looking at  minimum one but probably more like four  initiatives in November 2012 all right  Rob Campea of Marijuana Policy Project  MPP you're at MPP org thank you very  much for talking to us thank you for  reason TV I'm Nick Gillespie
 

